keywords:
  - "turbomachinery"
  - "tip-leakage"
  - "labyrinth-seal"
  - "carbide-piston-ring"
  - "blade-tip"
  - "compressible-flow"
  - "cad-step"
  - "thermal-expansion"
  - "stage-efficiency"
abstract: >-
  Comparative boundary-representation (B-Rep) CAD modeling, +500 K differential
  thermal stack-up verification, compressible tip-leakage mass-flow sweep, and
  stage power-loss analysis evaluating a zero-hole six-tooth labyrinth shroud
  coupled with a pressure-energized split tungsten-carbide piston ring (Track A)
  against an unshrouded open-tip blade profile with twenty-two 2.20 mm cross-bleed
  ports (Track B). At stage pressure ratio PR = 2.0, inlet total temperature 800 K,
  and 25.0 kg/s core flow, Track A reduces tip-leakage mass flow by 99.25% baseline
  (96.98% degraded lift-off) and recovers 115.54 kW in direct bypass power
  (191.21 kW when accounting for tip-vortex and bleed-jet mixing at k_mix = 1.65).
